     "note": "Walk 1.83 m wide, its inner edge 0.25 m off the lot line, its deck 0.11 m over the ground, 0.055 m boards at a 0.32 m pitch — street stock, wider than the 0.26 m trim on the two inns' own walks — laid ACROSS the way a foot travels, HELD BETWEEN TWO STRING PIECES that run along it (T-0460): 0.09 m stock taking the outermost 0.09 m of the walk's own width on each side, its top flush with the boards it holds and its foot at the lowest ground under its own length, so the walk presents one made face to the road instead of a row of board ends standing over it. The boards carry no underside: two triangles a board, facing the earth they are laid on, on tens of thousands of them. The string pieces are cut at a 2.08 m bay rather than under every board: a town's worth of sidewalk is tens of thousands of small boxes, an edge timber to a board is a quarter of the walk's silhouette and three quarters of its cost, and the ground these streets cross is flat enough (the march refuses any stretch that rolls more than 0.07 m inside a walking deck) that a bay-length piece meets it as closely as a board-length one did. Every board in a bay takes THAT BAY's height rather than sampling the terrain under its own centre, which is what makes the top of the string piece and the tops of the boards it holds one line. Crossings 1.83 m wide and 6 boards laid ALONG the run. Fences 1.37 m tall — 4 ft 6 in, a street fence and not the Sauganash's private six-foot yard wall — of 0.305 m boards butted at 0.006 m on 2 stringers, posts every 2.44 m. Hitching posts 1.3 m tall and 0.16 m square under a 0.22 m capped head — the Sauganash's own numbers (T-0090, docs/LIBERTIES.md L136) carried across unchanged — standing 0.9 m beyond the walk's outer edge, in the same verge that hotel's own posts stand in. Not one of those numbers is a record's; they are how the layer is DRAWN."
   },
   "rule": {
-    "note": "A block face carries a walk iff it fronts one of the covered streets, its own block is IMPROVED — a committed footprint stands somewhere on the block's own ground, asked of the block boundary and not of its lots so that the PUBLIC SQUARE, which this plat cuts into no lots at all, keeps the walks its jail and estray pen earn; because an unimproved block is open prairie and the town did not plank prairie (T-1707, the clause the street fence and the hitching post already carried and this rule did not) — and a walk at its lot line still clears that street's own travelled track by 0.35 m. The face is then MARCHED in 5.2 m steps, and a step carries boards iff the ground under it stands at least 0.15 m over datum, rolls no more than 0.07 m (which is what lets the whole step be ONE deck a visitor stands on), and carries no committed footprint. Consecutive carrying steps become a run; a run under 10.4 m is a landing rather than a sidewalk and is dropped. A crossing exists only where two runs stop either side of one corridor. A lot gets a street fence iff it is improved and its nearest committed wall stands 3.0 m or more back from its own frontage line — where it does not, the building is the street wall. A FRONTAGE GETS A HITCHING POST iff a committed building stands on its lot, that building's trade is one this project already rules takes its custom from a stranger off the street (PUBLIC_TRADES in tools/generate_business_signboards.py — a works or a warehouse took carts at a yard gate and is refused in writing), the walk was actually laid in front of it, and the post's own stand is dry committed ground clearing the track by 0.35 m. It stands at 0.28 of the BUILDING's own frontage — not the lot's, because two trades can share a lot — and 2.98 m out from the lot line. THE STREET EDGE IS DEALT BY BUSINESS (T-1813): FRONTAGE_BY_BUSINESS reads each front's trade as an INN (two posts at the thirds of the front, a stoop at the door, a mounting block in the verge between the posts), a STORE (one post and a stoop), a FORWARDING house or warehouse (a wagon apron of heavy plank across the verge; the counting room keeps its post) or a WORKS (a tie rail in the verge). A stoop needs the front wall at the door to stand at least 0.6 m and under 3.0 m back of the walk; an apron needs 0.9 m of verge inside the track margin. THE WALK ITSELF IS DEALT BY THE SAME TABLE (T-1814): an inn and a store front the 1.83 m board walk; a forwarding house fronts a DECKED walk, the board walk widened by 1.22 m of 75 mm plank on its street side for the length of its own frontage wherever the walk is laid there, with its posts and apron standing out past the deck; a works fronts BARE GROUND, every march step whose middle lies in front of it refused, so the walk stops either side and its tie rail stands on the ground. A RECONSTRUCTED TRADE TAKES ITS FITTINGS AT ITS OWN TIER: the trade's grade no longer refuses a post (T-1052's clause 3, overruled by T-1211's 'every business face … attested, inferred, reconstructed'), and every post and fitting carries `trade_confidence` so the card says which trades the evidence holds. BEYOND THE COVERED STREETS A FACE IS LAID FRONTS-ONLY (T-1823): a business that stands on the block, faces the face within 45 deg and stands within 10 m of its lot line takes its walk, posts and fittings over its own frontage and half a march step past each end (and one more clear step where that is under 10.4 m), and nothing is laid between businesses or behind a street fence. Every refusal below names the clause that refused it. Read them in tools/generate_frontage_works.py.",
