een the posts), a STORE (one post and a stoop), a FORWARDING house or warehouse (a wagon apron of heavy plank across the verge; the counting room keeps its post), a WORKS (a tie rail in the verge) or a CHURCH (a stoop at its door and no post, T-3360). A stoop needs the front wall at the door to stand at least 0.6 m and under 3.0 m back of the walk; an apron needs 0.9 m of verge inside the track margin. THE WALK ITSELF IS DEALT BY THE SAME TABLE (T-1814): an inn and a store front the 1.83 m board walk; a forwarding house fronts a DECKED walk, the board walk widened by 1.22 m of 75 mm plank on its street side for the length of its own frontage wherever the walk is laid there, with its posts and apron standing out past the deck; a works fronts BARE GROUND, every march step whose middle lies in front of it refused, so the walk stops either side and its tie rail stands on the ground. A RECONSTRUCTED TRADE TAKES ITS FITTINGS AT ITS OWN TIER: the trade's grade no longer refuses a post (T-1052's clause 3, overruled by T-1211's 'every business face … attested, inferred, reconstructed'), and every post and fitting carries `trade_confidence` so the card says which trades the evidence holds. BEYOND THE COVERED STREETS A FACE IS LAID FRONTS-ONLY (T-1823): a business that stands on the block, faces the face within 45 deg and stands within 10 m of its lot line takes its walk, posts and fittings over its own frontage and half a march step past each end (and one more clear step where that is under 10.4 m), and nothing is laid between businesses or behind a street fence. Every refusal below names the clause that refused it. Read them in tools/generate_frontage_works.py.",
