+          "label": "Decision",
+          "text": "The closed draw of the 1834 Dearborn Street drawbridge lies level with its approaches, by the owner's ruling. The owner, 2026-11-03, on the ridge the draw then rose to at mid-river: *\"when the bridge was down did it really have a peak in the center like this, it seems very odd to me that would be the case\"*. This replaces the 1.2 m centre rise earlier work read from the pictures (“Dearborn’s pictured closed draw and piled caps”). **Why the picture is not followed here.** The rise rests on one composition drawn twice: Braunhold's plate in Andreas 1884 p. 631 and the Baker-Co engraving share their schooner, lighthouse and viewpoint, so they are not two witnesses. Both were made more than forty years after the bridge was chopped up in 1839. No text gives a rise: Andreas has a draw \"hoisted\" between \"gallows\" frames, and Moses and Kirkland 1895 p. 95 \"a double draw\". The bridge carried the town's main street and its teams, and 1.2 m over a 9.14 m leaf is a grade of about 1 in 7.6 each way, with the leaves' joint at its crest. A leaf hinged at its frame and lowered onto its bearing lies at the bearing's height. The drawings' rise reads as the engraver's licence or as leaves caught part-way up."
