+      "note": "OWNER RULING — T-3361, 2026-11-03. The owner, on the closed draw rising to a ridge at mid-river: “when the bridge was down did it really have a peak in the center like this, it seems very odd to me that would be the case”. Withdrawn and laid level. The rise rests on one composition only: Braunhold’s plate in Andreas 1884 p. 631 and the Baker-Co engraving (credited to the Chicago Tribune, 15 February 1880, original unverified), which share their schooner, lighthouse and viewpoint and so are one composition drawn twice, not two witnesses — both made more than forty years after the bridge was chopped up in 1839 by artists who never saw it. No text gives a rise: Andreas has a draw ‘hoisted’ by ‘gallows’ frames, Moses and Kirkland 1895 p. 95 ‘a double draw’, and neither says the leaves stood above the approaches when down. Against it is the bridge’s use: it carried the town’s main street and its teams, and 1.2 m over a 9.14 m leaf is a 1-in-7.6 grade each way with the leaves’ joint at its crest. A hinged leaf lowered onto its bearing lies at the bearing’s height, and the drawings’ rise reads as the engraver’s or as leaves caught part-way up. So the closed draw is laid level with the approaches. This departs from the pictures on purpose and both match records carry it as an `owner ruling` difference. The earlier reading is kept below as history. SUPERSEDED (T-2375, 2026-10-29): The retrospective Andreas 1884 p. 631 plate shows a central rise in the closed draw and boarded parapet. This supports qualitative form, not a surveyed profile, a geographic coordinate or an attested operating mechanism. The exact rise and folded timber joints are separately reconstructed. The same native pure profile drives the mesh and nominal walk surface."
