+    "local_e": 317.582,
+    "local_n": -90.651,
     "rotation_deg": 180.0,
     "position_confidence": "inferred",
     "position_sources": [
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@
       "drloih_hotels",
       "osm_streets_2026"
     ],
-    "position_note": "PLACED 2026-08-11. THE CORNER IS DOCUMENTED TWICE IN ANDREAS, IN TWO DIFFERENT CHAPTERS, AND THE COORDINATE IS DERIVED - so the tag follows the weaker half. 'a new house on the northwest corner of Wells and Lake streets' (hotel chapter) and 'another house on the northwest corner of Wells and Lake streets' (life of Mark Beaubien). drloih_hotels agrees. METHOD, the project's standard one: Lake Street and Wells Street both survive on their 1830 plat lines, and the modern West Lake Street / North Wells Street intersection centre was read from OpenStreetMap at EPSG:26916 E 447404.02, N 4637283.94 (OSM node 258957316, fetched 2026-08-11). The building's south face is set on the Lake Street frontage 12.2 m north of that centre and its east face on the Wells frontage 12.2 m west of it, 12.2 m being half of an 80 ft street as annotated on Hathaway 1834; the stored coordinate is the polygon origin AFTER the 180 degree facade bearing, which under the renderer's convention (renderers/web/js/walker.js) puts it at the building's north-east corner. Currey has the 1830 Thompson plat laying streets 'uniformly 66 feet wide', which would move each offset 2.1 m - the discrepancy recorded in docs/RESEARCH/hogan_store.md section 5. WORKING UNCERTAINTY ABOUT 20 M, which is the georeference's residual (data/datum.json: 17.5 m RMS on Wright 1834), not an additional guess. Facade bearing 180, due south onto Lake Street. THAT BEARING IS THE WEAKEST THING HERE: no source says which street the house fronted, and a corner house whose name is an EXCHANGE and whose office booked stage seats could as well have turned its front to Wells. Lake is chosen because it was one of the town's two principal thoroughfares and because the building's neighbours on this record's own street line - the Green Tree three blocks west, the Sauganash two blocks west - front their long side to Lake. THE GROUND UNDER IT IS MODELLED and the building lands on it within the walker's 0.35 m step-up rule, so the phase carries no ground_contact declaration. Note that this footprint would have sat exactly on the OLD box edge - local E +305.1 to +319.1 against an edge at +320 - and that the e1834_harbor_cut heightfield has since been extended to E +1700, which is what puts real terrain under the rest of this parcel too.\n\nTHE CORNER IS RULED, 2026-09-26 (T-1604), AND THIS POSITION DOES NOT MOVE. T-1600 recorded a conflict on this record the day before — that Russell E. Heacock's advertisement puts this house one block west, at Lake and Franklin — and the conflict does not survive being read against the claim it came from. The advertisement is chicago_democrat_1835_07_08#c012, and what it places is HEACOCK'S OWN OFFICE and not this building: '[Office in the 2d st]ory, opposite the Ex[cha]n[ge], [corne]r of Lake and Franklin-sts.' The corner clause is the advertiser's address — the one thing a notice like this exists to give — and the Exchange stands in it as the landmark opposite. NOTE ALSO WHAT THE PAPER PRINTS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT: the surviving type reads 'the Ex[cha]n[ge]', not 'the Exchange Coffee House'; the longer form was the reader's expansion, and the note T-1600 wrote quoted the expansion back as though it were the page. THIS PROJECT'S OWN REGISTER ALREADY READ IT THE RULED WAY, which is what settles it: the claim's `business.placement` records `class: corner` with `anchor: 'the Exchange'`, and data/businesses/biz_russell_e_heacock.json carries one anchored location at the franklin+lake crossing with `structure_id: null` and the register's `action: new_building` — Heacock at that corner, in no roof this town holds. So the disagreement was between two READINGS of one advertisement, not between two sources about one building, and Andreas's north-west corner of Wells and Lake — twice, in two chapters, with drloih_hotels agreeing — is unopposed. WHAT THE RULING COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN TIDIED: 'opposite' must then reach from the Franklin corner to this house, about one block of Lake Street. It reaches if Heacock's second story stood on the SOUTH side of Lake at Franklin, because the block face across Lake from there runs Franklin to Wells and this house holds its east end; it does not reach if he stood on the north side, and then 'opposite' is the loose usage for the way across the street. Nothing in the notice says which side he was on and nothing here chooses — both readings leave this corner where Andreas put it, which is why the residual does not touch the coordinate. THE TAG IS UNCHANGED AT `inferred` AND THE SOURCE LIST IS UNCHANGED: the ruling removes an objection and adds no witness to the coordinate, which is still derived from a modern intersection centre. The advertisement is evidence about Heacock's office, and it is cited on that record.",
