-    "position_note": "PLACED 2026-08-11. THE CORNER IS DOCUMENTED AND AGREED BY TWO INDEPENDENT PERIOD TEXTS; THE COORDINATE IS DERIVED, so the tag follows the weaker half. Andreas, p. 635: 'situated then on the northwest corner of Lake and Dearborn streets'. The Chicago Tribune letter of 2 February 1874, transcribed on chicagology_prefire021: 'In 1833 there was built upon the northwest corner of Lake and Dearborn streets an unpretending frame structure known as the Tremont House.' Chicago Illustrated of January 1866, on the same page, gives 'the north-west corner of Lake and Dearborn streets' a third time. DO NOT USE THAT PAGE'S HEADER, which says 'SE corner of Lake and Dearborn Streets' - data/sources/chicagology_prefire021.json records that the header describes the LATER Tremonts (the 1850 brick house by Van Osdel) and contradicts every body text on its own page. Andreas confirms the move: the house begun in December 1839 was built 'on the corner where the present Tremont stands', which is a different corner from the one that burned. METHOD, the project's standard one: Lake Street and Dearborn Street both survive on their 1830 plat lines, and the modern West Lake Street / North Dearborn Street intersection centre was read from OpenStreetMap at EPSG:26916 E 447772.72, N 4637283.04 (OSM node 27477597, fetched 2026-08-11 - the same node and the same figures data/structures/dole_warehouse_south.json used). The building's south face is set on the Lake Street frontage 12.2 m north of that centre and its east face on the Dearborn frontage 12.2 m west of it, 12.2 m being half of an 80 ft street as annotated on Hathaway 1834; the stored coordinate is the polygon origin AFTER the 180 degree facade bearing, which under the renderer's convention (renderers/web/js/walker.js) puts it at the building's north-east corner. Currey has the 1830 Thompson plat laying streets 'uniformly 66 feet wide', which would move each offset 2.1 m; that discrepancy is already recorded in docs/RESEARCH/hogan_store.md section 5. WORKING UNCERTAINTY ABOUT 20 M, which is the georeference's residual (data/datum.json: 17.5 m RMS on Wright 1834, 3.7 per cent paper stretch), not an additional guess. Facade bearing 180, due south onto Lake Street, which is an inference from the street and not a statement: no source says which way the house fronted, and the corner would equally admit a Dearborn Street front. THE GROUND UNDER IT IS NOW MODELLED: the e1834_harbor_cut heightfield was extended east and its box now runs local E -320..+1700 and N -400..+400 (data/terrain/epochs/e1834_harbor_cut/heightfield.json), so this footprint sits on real terrain and the validator measures its contact rather than clamping at a box edge. It lands within the walker's 0.35 m step-up rule, so the phase carries no ground_contact declaration. That is new as of this parcel: docs/LIBERTIES.md L40 - two thirds of the town standing on ground that had not been built - is already in that document's Resolved section, and this record is written on the far side of that resolution: no Lake Street building in this parcel needed a ground_contact declaration.",
