-    "note": "Andreas calls it a hotel in terms - 'Mr. and Mrs. Murphy took charge of this NEW HOTEL, which they christened the Exchange Coffee House' - and lists it in his index among the early hotels. A coffee house of this period is a public house that lodges and feeds people, not a modern cafe. THE STAGE OFFICE IS THE PART THIS PROJECT HAS BEEN GETTING SLIGHTLY WRONG, AND THE CORRECTION IS A YEAR. data/exclusions.json excludes a separate Frink & Walker stage office on the ground that 'in 1835-36 stage seats were taken at Markle's Exchange Coffee House at Lake and Wells', and docs/research/04-structures-south.md line 144 says the same. The sentence behind it is Andreas on Dr John T. Temple's stage line: 'An advertisement that appeared in the American on AUGUST 6, 1836, specifies that John T. Temple & Co. are proprietors of a stage line from Chicago to Peoria ... and that seats can be taken at Markle's Exchange Coffee House.' That is thirteen months AFTER the scene date. Temple's line itself is older - it carried the mail to Ottawa from 1 January 1834 - so seats were being sold somewhere in 1835; nothing reached says where. The exclusion of a separate stage-office BUILDING stands on its own feet, since no such building is attested before 1840. What does not stand is the claim that this house was the stage office in 1835, and this record does not make it.\n\nTHE SCENE-YEAR PRESS ATTESTS THE PUBLIC ROOM DIRECTLY, WHERE ANDREAS ONLY RECALLS THE HOUSE. 'a large and respectable Meeting of the Democratic friends of the National Administration of the town of Chicago, was hel[d] at the Ex[change] Coffee House in said town, on Tuesday Evening, April 13, 1835' — the Chicago Democrat of 20 May 1835, page 1 column 3, claim chicago_democrat_1835_05_20#c001, printing the proceedings with Col. T. J. V. Owen calling the meeting to order and Hiram Hugunin in the chair. The secretaries and the committee lists are not repeated here: the reading mints no one off them, and a name written into the town on a building's record is a name the inferred-household deal may no longer use. The same party met here again for the town-election caucus of 6 June 1835 and had moved to the Eagle Coffee House, Trowbridge's, by 24 June. So the house is in business either side of the scene date and its largest room is the town's political one in the spring of 1835, which is a use a tavern_inn function covers and an innkeeping one does not say.\nAND THE ADVERTISEMENT IN THE FOLLOWING WEEK'S PAPER NAMES THIS HOUSE AS A LANDMARK, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT IT DOES TO IT. Russell E. Heacock advertises an office '[in the 2d st]ory, opposite the Ex[cha]n[ge], [corne]r of Lake and Franklin-sts' (chicago_democrat_1835_07_08#c012). Read once as putting THIS house at Lake and Franklin, that looked like a conflict with Andreas's corner and was recorded as one on 2026-09-26 (T-1600). T-1604 ruled it the next day: the corner in the notice is Heacock's own address and the Exchange is the landmark opposite it, which is how the claim's own placement block and the business register had read it all along. The position does not move, and the reasoning — including what the ruling leaves unresolved about the word 'opposite' — is in position.note above.\n\nAND THE TRUSTEES SAT HERE ON 3 NOVEMBER 1834 (T-1591). The ordinance 'for the further prevention of Fires' was adopted 'At a meeting of the President and Trustees of the Town of Chicago, held at “the Exchange,” in said town, on the 3d day of November A. D. 1834', and the Chicago Democrat printed it three times: claims chicago_democrat_1834_11_05#c001, chicago_democrat_1834_11_12#c001 and chicago_democrat_1834_12_03#c001 \u2014 the third of them the clearest. The first printing loses the house's name under a letter list woven through its column; the second and third carry it whole, and that is where the first's supply comes from. So this building held a sitting of the town's government on a named day eight months before the scene date, and it was a different house from the one the trustees had used three weeks earlier. WHAT IS NOT ASSERTED IS THE ORDINANCE — carrying fire from one house to another without care or caution, five dollars a breach, in force from 12 November 1834. That is town law and it places nothing. IT ALSO GIVES NO CORNER: all three printings say 'in said town' and no more, so the conflict this record already states between Andreas's north-west corner of Wells and Lake and Heacock's own Lake-and-Franklin advertisement is neither settled nor touched by them."
