-    "note": "A tavern from 1829 and a hotel from 1831; the social centre of the settlement in the target window. Chicago's first billiard table in a public house stood here.\n\nTHE HOUSE IS OPEN, AND FRESHLY DONE UP, THREE WEEKS BEFORE THE SCENE DATE. Reviewing the town's public houses against Detroit's new American Hotel, the Chicago Democrat of 10 June 1835, page 2 column 5, claim chicago_democrat_1835_06_10#c001, reports that 'The Sauganash has been re[paired,] fitted up and furnished with neatness and [taste,] and so far as it goes, will[, we have no doubt,] be found just the thing'. That is the only dated statement of this building's CONDITION the corpus holds, and it bears on how the scene should read it: on 1835-07-01 the Sauganash is a working, recently refitted public house and not the shabby survivor its 1829 log origins invite. 'so far as it goes' is the editor's qualification and it means small — his complaint in the same paragraph is that no establishment in Chicago is 'sufficiently extensive'. The refit is recorded here rather than in the form block below because nothing in it names a fabric, a storey or a colour, and inventing one from 'fitted up' is exactly the supply this project refuses."
